#statement("Exercise")[
Let $K$ be a field, and let $n$ be a positive integer. Let $K\(x_1\,dots.h.c\,x_n\)$ be the field of rational functions over $K$ with $n$ variables, and let $L = K\[x_1\,dots.h.c\,x_n\,x_1^(- 1)\,dots.h.c\,x_n^(- 1)\]$ be the subring of $K\(x_1\,dots.h.c\,x_n\)$. Let $R = K\[x_1\,dots.h.c\,x_n\,y_1\,dots.h.c\,y_n\]$

+ For an element $p in R$, let $phi\(p\)$ denote the element of $L$ obtained by substituting $x_i^(- 1)$ into each variable $y_i$ in $p$. This map $phi : R arrow.r L$ is a ring homomorphism. Show that for an ideal $J$ of $L$, $phi^(- 1)\(J\)$ is an ideal of $R$

+ For $1 lt.eq i lt.eq n$, let $g_i = x_i y_i - 1$. Let #display-equation($R' = {r in R mid(bar.v) upright("for ") 1 lt.eq i lt.eq n upright(", every monomial in ") r upright(" does not involve ") x_i upright(" and ") y_i upright(" simultaneously")}$) Show that for an arbitrary element $p in R$, there exist $h_1\,dots.h.c\,h_n in R$ and $r in R'$ such that $p = h_1 g_1 + dots.h.c + g_n h_n + r$

+ Let $I$ denote the ideal of $R$ generated by $g_1\,dots.h.c\,g_n$. Show that $ker phi = I$ and that $L$ is isomorphic to the quotient ring $R\/I$

]
#proof(title: "Solution")[
+ By definition

+ Suppose monomial $q$ containing factor $\(x_1 y_1\)^k$ but not $\(x_1 y_1\)^(k + 1)$ , since $\(x_1 y_1\)^k =\(g_1 + 1\)^k$, $q$ can be written as $u g_1 + v$, where every monomial in $v$ does not involve $x_1$ and $y_1$ simultaneously. Repeat for $g_2\,dots.h.c\,g_n$, then we are done

+ $phi\(g_i\)= 0 arrow.r.double I lt.eq ker phi$, conversely, if $p in ker phi$, then $0 = phi\(r\)arrow.r.double r = 0$, thus $ker phi = I$. Since $phi$ is surjective, by first isomorphism theorem, we have $L tilde.equiv R\/I$

]
