]
#statement("Exercise")[
$U subset.eq Omega subset.eq bb(C)$ are open, $f$ is holomorphic on $U$, $hat(U)_Omega$ be the union of $U$ and compact connected components of $Omega\\U$. There exist ${ f_n }$ holomorphic on $Omega$ converging uniformly to $f$ on compact subsets of $U$ iff there exists $g$ holomorphic on $H\(hat(U)_Omega\)$ such that $g\|_U = f$

]
#proof(title: "Solution")[
Assume $hat(U)_Omega = U union K_1 union dots.h.c$, where $K_i$'s are compact

Suppose ${ f_n }$ holomorphic on $Omega$ converging uniformly to $f$ on compact subsets of $U$, by maximum principle, ${ f_n }$ would be uniformly bounded around $K_i$, by Montel's theorem #ref(label("Montel's theorem"), supplement: none), there exists a subsequence of ${ f_n }$ converges uniformly on $K_i$, thus converging to $g$ holomorphic on $H\(hat(U)_Omega\)$, hence $g\|_U = f$

Conversely, suppose $g$ holomorphic on $H\(hat(U)_Omega\)$ such that $g\|_U = f$, $hat(U)_Omega$ is simply connected, by Riemann mapping theorem #ref(label("Riemann mapping theorem"), supplement: none), we can think of $hat(U)_Omega$ as the unit disc or $bb(C)$, by Runge's theorem, there exist ${ f_n }$ holomorphic on $Omega$ uniformly converging to $g$ on each disc. Thus there exist a subsequence of ${ f_n }$ converging uniformly to $g$ on compact subsets of $hat(U)_Omega$

]
#statement("Exercise")[
Let $Omega$ be an open subset of $bb(C)$, $cal(D) = { D_i }$ be an open cover of $Omega$ with disks. Given meromorphic functions $h_i$ on $D_i$, not identically zero. Assume $g_(i j) = h_i / h_j$ are holomorphic on $D_i inter D_j$, then there exist holomorphic function $f_i$ with no zeros on $D_i$ such that $f_i = g_(i j) f_j$

]
#proof(title: "Solution")[
It suffices to prove $H^1\(Omega\,cal(O)^(*)\)= 0$, since then $H^1\(cal(D)\,cal(O)^(*)\)= 0$, $\(g_(i j)\)in Z^1\(cal(D)\,cal(O)^(*)\)= B^1\(cal(D)\,cal(O)^(*)\)$, i.e. there exists $\(f_i\)in C^0\(cal(D)\,cal(O)^(*)\)$ such that $f_i = g_(i j) f_j$

Consider exact sequence of sheaves $0 arrow.r bb(Z) arrow.r.hook cal(O) arrow.r^exp cal(O)^(*) arrow.r 0$, then we get a long exact sequence $dots.h.c arrow.r H^1\(Omega\,cal(O)\)arrow.r H^1\(Omega\,cal(O)^(*)\)arrow.r H^2\(Omega\,bb(Z)\)arrow.r dots.h.c$, $H^1\(Omega\,cal(O)\)= 0$ by Mittag-Leffler theorem

]
#statement("Exercise")[
For each real $r$ such that $0 <\|r\|< 1$, prove that there exists at most one real $s$ with $0 < s < 1$ for which $Omega := D\\{ 0\,r\,s }$ admits an analytic automorphism different from the identity

]
#proof(title: "Solution")[
Suppose $Omega arrow.r^phi.alt Omega$ is an analytic automorphism, then $0\,r\,s$ are all removable singularities, by continuity, $phi.alt$ can be extended to $D arrow.r^phi.alt D$, so is $phi.alt^(- 1)$, by continuity, we know $phi.alt$ is an automorphism of $D$, sending ${ 0\,r\,s }$ to itself bijectively

By Schwarz lemma, we know that an automorphism $phi.alt$ of $D$ with $phi.alt\(alpha\)= 0$ iff $phi.alt = e^(i theta) frac(z - alpha, 1 - overline(alpha) z)$

Now suppose $phi.alt$ is an automorphism different from the identity, if $0 < r = s < 1$, then $phi.alt = - frac(z - r, 1 - r z)$ is a choice, now we assumen $r eq.not s$

+ $phi.alt\(0\)= 0$

  $phi.alt = e^(i theta) z$, $phi.alt\(r\)= s$, but $0 < s < 1$, thus $s =\|r\|$

+ $phi.alt\(r\)= 0$

  $phi.alt = e^(i theta) frac(z - r, 1 - overline(r) z)$, $phi.alt\(0\)= - r e^(i theta)$

  + $theta = pi$, $phi.alt\(0\)= r$, then $s = phi.alt\(s\)arrow.r.double overline(r) s^2 - 2 s + r = 0 arrow.r.double s = frac(1 + sqrt(1 -\|r\|^2), overline(r)) upright(" or ") frac(1 - sqrt(1 -\|r\|^2), overline(r))$, $r$ has to be a positive real number and $s = frac(1 - sqrt(1 - r^2), r)$

  + $s = phi.alt\(0\)=\|r\|$

+ $phi.alt\(s\)= 0$

  $phi.alt = e^(i theta) frac(z - s, 1 - s z)$, $phi.alt\(0\)= - s e^(i theta)$

  + $theta = pi$, $phi.alt\(0\)= s$, then $r = phi.alt\(r\)arrow.r.double s r^2 - 2 r + s = 0 arrow.r.double s = frac(2 r, 1 + r^2)$.

  + $r = phi.alt\(0\)= - s e^(i theta)$, $s = phi.alt\(r\)arrow.r.double s^2 = 1$ which is impossible

]
#statement("Exercise")[
$F subset.eq bb(C)$ is closed, connected and noncompact, $Omega = bb(C)\\F$, then every $f in cal(O)\(Omega\)$ has a primitive

]
#proof(title: "Solution")[
It suffices to show that every connected component $U$ of $Omega$ is simply connected

Suppose $U$ is not simply connected, then $pi_1\(U\,z_0\)eq.not 0$, i.e. there is a simple(self non-intersecting) loop $gamma subset.eq U$ with $gamma\(0\)= gamma\(1\)$ cannot be deform to $z_0$, by Jordan curve theorem #ref(label("Jordan curve theorem"), supplement: none), $gamma$ divides $bb(C)$ into the exterior and the interior which is homeomorphic to the unit disc $D$, suppose $F inter D$ is empty, then $overline(D) subset.eq U$, $gamma$ can be deformed to $z_0$, giving a contradiction, hence $F inter overline(D)$ is a compact connected component of $F$ which is also a contradiction

]
#statement("Exercise")[
Consider an open set $Omega subset.eq bb(C)^2$ such that #display-equation(${\(z\,w\)in bb(C)^2\|\|z\|lt.eq R_1\,\|w\|lt.eq R_2 } subset.eq Omega$) for some positive reals $R_1$ and $R_2$. Let $f in cal(H)\(Omega\)$ be such that $f\(z\,w\)eq.not 0$ for every $z$ and $w$ for which $\|z\|lt.eq R_1$, $\|w\|= R_2$

+ Prove that the number (counted with multiplicities) of zeros of $w mapsto f\(z\,w\)$ in $D\(0\,R_2\)$ is the same for every $\|z\|lt.eq R_1$

+ Let $w_1\(z\)\,dots.h.c\,w_m\(z\)$ denote the zeros of $w mapsto f\(z\,w\)$ (counted with multiplicities). Prove that for each $n in bb(N)$ the function #display-equation($z mapsto w_1\(z\)^n + dots.h.c + w_m\(z\)^n$) is holomorphic for $z in D\(0\,R_1\)$

+ Deduce that $n$th elementary symmetric function $sigma_n$ of $w_1\(z\)\,dots.h.c\,w_m\(z\)$ is holomorphic.

+ Prove that there exists a function $h$ that is holomorphic and without any zeros on ${\(z\,w\)in bb(C)^2\|\|z\|< R_1\,\|w\|< R_2 }$ such that #display-equation($f\(z\,w\)= h\(z\,w\)\[w^m + sigma_1\(z\)w^(m - 1) + dots.h.c + sigma_(m - 1)\(z\)w + sigma_m\(z\)\]$) for every $z$ and $w$ such that $\|z\|< R_1$ and $\|w\|< R_2$

]
#proof(title: "Solution")[
+ By Lemma #ref(label("Lemma for finding zeros"), supplement: none), $frac(1, 2 pi i) integral_(partial D\(0\,R_2\)) frac(f_w\(z\,w\), f\(z\,w\)) d w$ is the number of zeros in $D\(0\,R_2\)$ which is continuous, hence the same for every $\|z\|lt.eq R_1$

+ By Lemma #ref(label("Lemma for finding zeros"), supplement: none), $frac(1, 2 pi i) integral_(partial D\(0\,R_2\)) w^n frac(f_w\(z\,w\), f\(z\,w\)) d w = w_1\(z\)^n + dots.h.c + w_m\(z\)^n$ is holomorphic

+ Directly follows from (2) thanks to Newton's identities

+ Since $product_(i = 1)^m\(w - w_i\(z\)\)= w^m + sigma_1\(z\)w^(m - 1) + dots.h.c + sigma_(m - 1)\(z\)w + sigma_m\(z\)$ is holomorphic #display-equation($frac(f\(z\,w\), w^m + sigma_1\(z\)w^(m - 1) + dots.h.c + sigma_(m - 1)\(z\)w + sigma_m\(z\))$) has no zeros on $D$ and holomorphic on ${ R_2 - epsilon <\|w\|< R_2 }$, hence by Hartogs's extension theorem #ref(label("Hartogs's extension theorem"), supplement: none), can be extended to a holomorphic function $h\(z\,w\)$, then $f\(z\,w\)= h\(z\,w\)\[w^m + sigma_1\(z\)w^(m - 1) + dots.h.c + sigma_(m - 1)\(z\)w + sigma_m\(z\)\]$ on ${ R_2 - epsilon <\|w\|< R_2 }$, by identity theorem, this holds for all $\|z\|< R_1$ and $\|w\|< R_2$

]
#statement("Exercise")[
Suppose $p_1\,dots.h.c\,p_n$ are points on the compact Riemann surface $X$ and $X' = X\\{ p_1\,dots.h.c\,p_n }$. Suppose $f : X' arrow.r bb(C)$ is a non-constant holomorphic function. Show that the image of $f$ comes arbitrarily close to every $c in bb(C)$

]
#proof(title: "Solution")[
Suppose there exists $c in bb(C)$ such that $\|f - c\|gt.eq epsilon$ for some $epsilon > 0$, then $frac(1, f - c)$ would be a bounded holomorphic function on $X'$, by Riemann's Removable singularity theorem, $frac(1, f - c)$ can be extended to a holomorphic function on $X$, but since $X$ is compact, $frac(1, f - c)$ is a constant which is impossible

]
#statement("Exercise")[
Let $X$ be a compact Riemann surface and let $X arrow.r^sigma X$ be a biholomorphic map of $X$ onto itself, different from the identity. Let $a in X$ be a point with $sigma\(a\)eq.not a$, and suppose that there is a non-constant meromorphic function $f$ on $X$, holomorphic on $X\\{ a }$, with a pole of order $k$ at $a$. Prove that $sigma$ can have at most $2 k$ fixed points on $X$

]
#proof(title: "Solution")[
Suppose there are more than $2 k$ fixed points of $sigma$, then consider $f - f compose sigma^(- 1) : X arrow.r bb(P)^1$ is holomorphic on $X\\{ a\,sigma^(- 1)\(a\)}$ with at least $2 k + 1$ zeros and with poles of order $k$ at $a\,sigma^(- 1)\(a\)$, but it should have as many poles as zeros which is a contradiction

]
