#statement("Exercise")[
$Lambda = bb(Z) omega_1 + bb(Z) omega_2$, $Lambda' = bb(Z) omega'_1 + bb(Z) omega'_2$ are lattices in $bb(C)$ and $X = bb(C)\/Lambda$, $X' = bb(C)\/Lambda'$ are the corresponding complex tori

+ Prove that any holomorphic map $X arrow.r^f X'$ is induced by a linear map $bb(C) arrow.r^g bb(C)$ of the form $g\(z\)= alpha z + beta$, where $alpha in bb(C)$ is such that $alpha Lambda subset.eq Lambda'$. $f$ is biholomorphic if and only if $alpha Lambda = Lambda'$

+ Show that every torus $X = bb(C)\/Lambda$ is isomorphic to a torus of the form $X\(tau\)= bb(C)\/\(bb(Z) + bb(Z) tau\)$, where $tau in bb(C)$ satisfies $upright(I m)\(tau\)> 0$

+ Assume that $(mat(delim:#none, a, b; c, d)) in upright(S L)\(2\,bb(Z)\)$ and $upright(I m)\(tau\)> 0$. Let $tau' := frac(a tau + b, c tau + d)$. Show that the tori $X\(tau\)$ and $X\(tau'\)$ are biholomorphic

]
#proof(title: "Solution")[
+ Since $bb(C)$ is the universal cover of $bb(C)\/Lambda'$, $f compose pi : bb(C) arrow.r bb(C)\/Lambda'$ has a lift $F : bb(C) arrow.r bb(C)$, and locally we have $F = pi'\|_V^(- 1) compose f compose pi\|_U$, thus $F$ is holomorphic

  #align(center)[
  #align(center)[#set text(size:9pt)
  #diagram(spacing:(7mm,9mm),
  node((1,0),[$bb(C)$]),
  node((2,0),[$bb(C)$]),
  node((1,1),[$bb(C)\/Lambda$]),
  node((2,1),[$bb(C)\/Lambda'$]),
  edge((1,0),(2,0),"->",[$F$]),
  edge((1,0),(1,1),"->",[$pi$]),
  edge((2,0),(2,1),"->",[$pi'$]),
  edge((1,1),(2,1),"->",[$f$]))]
  ]
  Fix $omega in Lambda$, since $pi\(z + omega\)= pi\(z\)$ for any $z in bb(C)$, we have $F\(z + omega\)- F\(z\)in Lambda'$, hence $F\(z + omega\)- F\(z\)$ is a continuous function of $z$ but $Lambda'$ is discrete, thus $F\(z + omega\)- F\(z\)equiv C_omega$, where $C_omega in Lambda'$ is a constant. Then $F'\(z + omega\)= F'\(z\)$ which shows $F' : bb(C) arrow.r bb(C)$ is doubly periodic function, thus induces $G : bb(C)\/Lambda arrow.r bb(C)$ with $F = G compose pi$. Thus $G$ must be a constant, so is $F'$, therefore $F$ has the form $F\(z\)= alpha z + beta$. Then for any $omega in Lambda$, we have $F\(omega\)- F\(0\)= alpha omega in Lambda'$, thus $alpha Lambda subset Lambda'$. If $f$ is biholomorphic, then $pi' compose F = f compose pi arrow.r.double pi compose F^(- 1) = f^(- 1) compose pi'$, which implies ${mat(delim:#none, alpha Lambda subset Lambda'; alpha^(- 1) Lambda' subset Lambda) arrow.r.double alpha Lambda = Lambda'$

  #align(center)[
  #align(center)[#set text(size:9pt)
  #diagram(spacing:(7mm,9mm),
  node((1,0),[$bb(C)$]),
  node((2,0),[$bb(C)$]),
