  edge((2,0),(2,1),"->",[$pi'$]),
  edge((2,1),(1,1),"->",[$f^(- 1)$]))]
  ]
  Conversely, if $alpha Lambda = Lambda'$, $pi compose F^(- 1)$ is doubly periodic and induce $f^(- 1)$, hence $f$ is biholomorphic

+ Suppose $Lambda = bb(Z) omega_1 + bb(Z) omega_2\,upright(I m) (omega_2 / omega_1) > 0$, define $Lambda' = bb(Z) + bb(Z) tau$, where $tau = omega_2 / omega_1$, we have $omega_1 Lambda' = Lambda$, thus $X$ and $X\(tau\)$ are biholomorphic

+ $X\(tau\)$ and $X\(tau'\)$ are biholomorphic iff $vec(tau', 1) = alpha A vec(tau, 1)\,alpha in bb(C) - { 0 }\,A in upright(S L)\(2\,bb(Z)\)$ If $X\(tau\)$ and $X\(tau'\)$ are biholomorphic, then $bb(Z) + bb(Z) tau' = Lambda' = alpha Lambda = bb(Z) alpha + bb(Z) alpha tau$ for some $alpha in bb(C) - { 0 }$, thus $vec(tau', 1) = A vec(alpha tau, alpha) = alpha A vec(tau, 1)$, for some $A in upright(S L)\(2\,bb(Z)\)$, the other direction is easy

]
