]
Let $Omega subset.eq bb(C)$ be a lattice and $℘\(z\)$ the associated Weierstrass $℘$-function. We have seen that $℘\(z\)$ satisfies the differential equation $\(℘'\(z\)\)^2 = p\(℘\(z\)\)$, where $p\(x\)= 4 x^3 - g_2 x - g_3$. The following three problems examine the conditions under which the coefficients $g_2$ and $g_3$ of $p\(x\)$ are real numbers

#statement("Exercise")[
Prove that the following conditions are equivalent

+ $g_2\,g_3 in bb(R)$

+ $G_k in bb(R)$ for all $k gt.eq 3$

+ $℘\(macron(z)\)= overline(℘\(z\))$ for all $z in bb(C)$

+ $overline(Omega) = Omega$ (the last condition says that $Omega$ is a #emph[real lattice])

]
#proof(title: "Solution")[
$\(i\)arrow.r.double\(i i\)$

$g_2 = 60 G_4\,g_3 = 140 G_6 in bb(R) arrow.r.double G_4\,G_6 in bb(R)$

Since #display-equation($℘\(z\) & = 1 / z^2 + sum_(n = 2)^oo\(2 n - 1\)G_(2 n) z^(2 n - 2)\
 & = 1 / z^2 + 3 G_4 z^2 + 5 G_6 z^4 + 7 G_8 z^6 + 9 G_10 z^8 + dots.h.c$) #display-equation($℘'\(z\) & = - 2 / z^3 + sum_(n = 2)^oo\(2 n - 1\)\(2 n - 2\)G_(2 n) z^(2 n - 3)\
 & = - 2 / z^3 + 6 G_4 z + 20 G_6 z^3 + 42 G_8 z^5 + 72 G_10 z^7 + dots.h.c$) #display-equation($℘''\(z\) & = 6 / z^4 + sum_(n = 2)^oo\(2 n - 1\)\(2 n - 2\)\(2 n - 3\)G_(2 n) z^(2 n - 4)\
 & = 6 / z^4 + 6 G_4 + 60 G_6 z^2 + 210 G_8 z^4 + 504 G_10 z^6 + dots.h.c$) So we can conclude $℘''\(z\)- 6 ℘\(z\)^2 + 30 G_4 = z phi\(z\)$, where $phi\(z\)$ is a holomorphic elliptic function, hence $℘''\(z\)- 6 ℘\(z\)^2 + 30 G_4 = 0$, then the coefficients of $z^(2 n)\(n gt.eq 1\)$ would be $\(2 n + 1\)\(2 n + 2\)\(2 n + 3\)\(2 n + 4\)G_(2 n + 4) - 6\(2 n + 3\)G_(2 n + 4)$ minus terms only involving $G_4\,G_6\,dots.h.c\,G_(2 n + 2)$ and real numbers, thus by induction, we know $G_(2 n + 4) in bb(R)\(n gt.eq 1\)$

$\(i i\)arrow.r.double\(i i i\)$

Since $℘\(z\)= 1 / z^2 + sum_(n = 2)^oo\(2 n - 1\)G_(2 n) z^(2 n - 2)$, if $G_k in bb(R)\(k gt.eq 3\)$, then $℘\(macron(z)\)= overline(℘\(z\))$

$\(i i i\)arrow.r.double\(i v\)$

The poles of $overline(℘\(macron(z)\)) = ℘\(z\)$ are exactly $overline(Omega)$, thus $overline(Omega) = Omega$

$\(i v\)arrow.r.double\(i\)$

$g_2 = 60 G_4 = 60 sum_(omega in Omega^(*)) 1 / omega^4 = 60 sum_(omega in overline(Omega)^(*)) 1 / omega^4 = overline(g_2) arrow.r.double g_2 in bb(R)$, similarly, $g_6 in bb(R)$

]
#statement("Exercise")[
We say that $Omega$ is #emph[real rectangular] if $Omega = bb(Z) omega_1 + bb(Z) omega_2$ where $omega_1 in bb(R)$ and $omega_2 in i bb(R)$, and that $Omega$ is #emph[real rhombic] if $Omega = bb(Z) omega_1 + bb(Z) omega_2$ where $omega_2 = overline(omega)_1$. Prove that a lattice $Omega$ is real if and only if it is real rectangular or real rhombic

]
#proof(title: "Solution")[
If $Omega$ is real rectangular or real rhombic, $Omega$ is obviously a real lattice

Conversely, if $Omega$ is a real lattice, suppose $Omega = bb(Z) omega_1 + bb(Z) omega_2$, then there exists $omega in Omega\\\(bb(R) union i bb(R)\)$, otherwise, $omega_1 in bb(R)^(*)\,omega_2 in i bb(R)^(*)$ or $omega_2 in bb(R)^(*)\,omega_1 in i bb(R)^(*)$, since $omega_1\,omega_2$ are linear independent, but then $omega = omega_1 + omega_2 in Omega\\\(bb(R) union i bb(R)\)$ which is a contradiction

Since $omega in Omega\\\(bb(R) union i bb(R)\)$, $omega + overline(omega) in bb(R)^(*)\,omega - overline(omega) in i bb(R)^(*)$, thus $Omega inter bb(R)^(*) eq.not diameter\,Omega inter i bb(R)^(*) eq.not diameter$, let $eta_1 = min_(eta in Omega inter\(0\,oo\)) eta$, then $Omega inter bb(R) = bb(Z) eta_1$, otherwise $exists eta in bb(R)\\bb(Z) eta_1$, then $eta - ⌊eta / eta_1⌋ eta_1 in Omega inter\(0\,oo\)$ which is a contradiction

Similarly, $Omega inter i bb(R) = bb(Z) eta_2$ for some $eta_2 in i\(0\,oo\)$. If $Omega = bb(Z) eta_1 + bb(Z) eta_2$, then $Omega$ is real rectangular, if not, $exists gamma in Omega\\\(bb(R) union i bb(R)\)$, such that $\|gamma\|= min_(omega in Omega\\\(bb(R) union i bb(R)\))\|omega\|$, then $gamma + overline(gamma) = eta_1$ or $- eta_1$, otherwise $gamma + overline(gamma) = k eta_1$ for some $\|k\|gt.eq 2$

If $k = 2$, then $gamma - eta_1 = eta_1 - overline(gamma) = - overline(\(gamma - eta_1\)) arrow.r.double gamma - eta_1 in i bb(R) arrow.r.double gamma in bb(Z) eta_1 + bb(Z)\(gamma - eta_1\)subset.eq bb(Z) eta_1 + bb(Z) eta_2$

If $k > 2$, then $gamma - eta_1 in.not bb(R) union i bb(R)$ and $\|gamma - eta_1\|<\|gamma\|$, similarly for $k lt.eq - 2$, these are all contradictions

Similarly, we know that $gamma - overline(gamma) = eta_2$ or $- eta_2$

Now, for any $omega in Omega\\\(bb(R) union i bb(R)\)$, $omega + overline(omega) = k eta_1 = k\(gamma + overline(gamma)\)$ for some $k eq.not 0$, then $omega - k gamma = k overline(gamma) - overline(omega) = - overline(\(omega - k gamma\)) arrow.r.double omega - k gamma in i bb(R)$, if $omega eq.not k gamma$, then $omega - k gamma = l eta_2 = l\(gamma - overline(gamma)\)arrow.r.double omega in bb(Z) gamma + bb(Z) overline(gamma)$, therefore, we have $Omega = bb(Z) gamma + bb(Z) overline(gamma)$, $Omega$ is real rhombic

]
#statement("Exercise")[
Let $Omega$ be a real lattice. Define the real elliptic curve $E_(bb(R))$ to be the set ${\(x\,y\)in bb(R)^2 med\|med y^2 = p\(x\)}$. Prove that $E_(bb(R))$ has one or two connected components as $Omega$ is real rhombic or real rectangular, respectively

]
#proof(title: "Solution")[
The number of connected components of $E_(bb(R))$ is one or two if $p\(x\)= 0$ has one real root and two nonreal conjugate complex roots or three distinct real roots correspondingly

Since $omega_1 / 2\,omega_2 / 2\,omega_3 / 2$ are simple roots of $℘'\(z\)$, the three simple roots of $p\(x\)$ are $℘ (omega_1 / 2)\,℘ (omega_2 / 2)\,℘ (omega_3 / 2)$, since $Omega$ is a real lattice, $G_k in bb(R)$ and $℘\(z\)= 1 / z^2 + sum_(n = 2)^oo\(2 n - 1\)G_(2 n) z^(2 n - 2)$

If $Omega$ is real rectangular, then $℘ (omega_1 / 2)\,℘ (omega_2 / 2)$ are both real, thus $E_(bb(R))$ has two connected components

If $Omega$ is real rhombic, then $℘ (omega_3 / 2)$ is real $℘ (omega_1 / 2) eq.not ℘ (omega_2 / 2)$ are nonreal conjugate, thus $E_(bb(R))$ has only one connected component

]
#statement("Exercise")[
$A\(r\,R\)= { r <\|z\|< R }$ is biholomorphic to ${ s <\|z\|< S }$ iff $R\/r = S\/s$, $r$ can be $0$, $R$ can be $oo$, but not at the same time

]#label("Complex structures on an open annulus")
#proof(title: "Solution")[
By scaling or inversion we can assume $r = s = 1$ and $\|f\(z\)\|arrow.r 1$ as $\|z\|arrow.r 1$. Suppose $f : A\(r\,R\)arrow.r A\(s\,S\)$ is a biholomorphism, then consider the Laurent series $f = sum_(k = - oo)^oo c_k z^k$, for $1 < t < R$, by Stokes theorem we have #display-equation($A\(t\) & = frac(1, 2 i) integral_(f\({\|z\|= t }\)) macron(z) d z = frac(1, 2 i) integral_(\|z\|= t) overline(f\(z\)) d f\(z\)= frac(1, 2 i) integral_(\|z\|= t) overline(f\(z\)) f'\(z\)d z = pi sum_(k in bb(Z)) k\|c_k\|^2 t^(2 k)$) As $t arrow.r 1$, we have $A\(t\)arrow.r pi arrow.r.double sum k\|c_k\|^2 = 1$, thus #display-equation($A\(t\)- pi t^2 = pi t^2 sum_(k in bb(Z)) k\|c_k\|^2 (t^(2 k - 2) - 1) gt.eq 0$) Thus $A\(t\)gt.eq pi t^2$, as $t arrow.r R$, $A\(t\)arrow.r pi S^2 gt.eq pi R^2 arrow.r.double S gt.eq R$. Therefore we have $S = R$

]
#statement("Exercise")[
Let $mu : cal(B)\(bb(R)\)arrow.r bb(R)^(+)$ be a $sigma$-additive set function defined on the Borel $sigma$-algebra on $bb(R)$ and let $D subset.eq bb(R)$ be a discrete set with the property $x in D$ if and only if there exists an open set $U$ such that $x in U$ and $mu\(U\)> 0$. Show that $mu$ can be expressed as a countable linear combination of measures of the form #display-equation($delta_x\(A\)= cases(1 quad x in A, 0 quad x in.not A)$) Where $x in D$ and $A in cal(B)\(bb(R)\)$

]
#proof(title: "Proof")[
For each $x in D$, denote $mu\({ x }\)= c_x$. Since $D$ is discrete, it is countable and any subset of $D$ is closed. For any open set $V$ in $bb(R)$, by definition, $mu\(V - D\)= 0$ since $V - D$ is an open set which doesn't intersect $D$. Hence #display-equation($mu\(V\)= mu\(V inter D\)+ mu\(V - D\)= mu\(V inter D\)= sum_(x in V inter D) c_x = sum_(x in D) c_x delta_x\(V\)$) i.e. $mu$ can be expressed as a countable linear combination of Dirac measures

]
#statement("Exercise")[
Let $c :\[0\,1\]arrow.r\[0\,1\]$ denote the Cantor (ternary) function (known also as the Devil's staircase, or the Cantor-Lebesgue function). For each continuous real-valued function $f :\[0\,1\]arrow.r bb(R)$, let $l\(f\)$ denote the Riemann integral #display-equation($l\(f\)= integral_0^1 f\(c\(x\)\)d x$) Find explicitly a Borel measure $mu : cal(B)\(\[0\,1\]\)arrow.r bb(R)$ so that #display-equation($l\(f\)= integral_(\[0\,1\]) f d mu$) where $cal(B)\(\[0\,1\]\)$ denotes the Borel $sigma$-algebra over $\[0\,1\]$

]
#proof(title: "Proof")[
Write $m$ as the Lebesgue measure. Since $c$ is a continuous function, the pushforward measure $mu\(E\)= m\(c^(- 1)\(E\)\)$ is a Borel measure on $cal(B)\(\[0\,1\]\)$. Note that #display-equation($integral_(\[0\,1\]) chi_E d mu = mu\(E\)= m\(c^(- 1)\(E\)\)= integral_0^1 chi_E\(c\(x\)\)d x$) and continuous functions are approximated by simple functions, thus #display-equation($integral_(\[0\,1\]) f d mu = integral_0^1 f\(c\(x\)\)d x$)

]
#statement("Exercise")[
Suppose $f\(z\)$ is a meromorphic function on $bb(C)$ with finitely many zeros and poles at ${ z_1\,dots.h.c\,z_N }$, i.e. e. $f\(z\)$ is holomorphic and nowhere vanishing on $bb(C)\\{ z_1\,dots.h.c\,z_N }$. Let $m_i in bb(Z)$ be the order of $f\(z\)$ at $z_i$. Furthermore, assume there is $A in bb(C)\\{ 0 }$ and a real number $C > 0$, such that #display-equation($\|f\(z\)- A\|lt.eq frac(C, \|z\|^2)$) for all $z$ with $\|z\|$ sufficiently large. Prove that #display-equation($sum_(i = 1)^N m_i z_i = 0$)

]
#proof(title: "Proof")[
Let $w = 1\/z$, $g\(w\)= f\(1\/w\)= f\(z\)$, then $\|g\(w\)- A\|lt.eq C\|w\|^2$, then $frac(g\(w\)- A, w^2)$ holomorphic and bounded around $0$, denote as $h\(w\)$, we have $g\(w\)= A + w^2 h\(w\)$, hence for $R$ large enough, $epsilon.alt = 1\/R$ small enough, we have #display-equation($sum_(i = 1)^N m_i z_i & = integral_(\|z\|= R) z frac(f'\(z\), f\(z\)) d z = integral_(\|w\|= epsilon.alt) frac(g'\(w\), w g\(w\)) d w = 0$) Since $frac(g'\(w\), w g\(w\))$ is holomorphic around $0$

]
