#statement("Exercise")[
Let ${ f_n }\,n gt.eq 1$ be a sequence in $L^2\(\[0\,1\]\)$ and $f$ be a Lebesgue measurable function such that for every Lebesgue measurable set $E$ in $\[0\,1\]$ #display-equation($integral_E f_n d x arrow.r integral_E f d x$) as $n arrow.r oo$. Assume also that $sup_n integral_0^1\|f_n\|^2 d x < oo$

+ Prove that $f in L^2\(\[0\,1\]\)$

+ Prove that for any function $g in L^2\(\[0\,1\]\)$ #display-equation($integral_0^1 f_n g d x arrow.r integral_0^1 f g d x$)

]
#proof(title: "Solution")[
+ $f_n$ converge to $f$ in measure since otherwise there would exists $epsilon.alt > 0$ and $E$ with $m\(E\)> 0$ such that $f_n - f gt.eq epsilon.alt$ on $E$, then #display-equation($integral_E\(f_n - f\)d x gt.eq epsilon.alt m\(E\)$) thus there is a subsequence $f_(n_k) arrow.r f$ almost everywhere, by Fatou's lemma #display-equation($integral_0^1\|f\|^2 d x lt.eq liminf_(k arrow.r oo) integral_0^1\|f_(n_k)\|^2 d x lt.eq sup_n integral_0^1\|f_n\|^2 d x < oo$)

+ Converges in measure $arrow.r.double$ converges weakly #display-equation($integral_0^1\|\(f_n - f\)g\|d x & = integral_(\|f_n - f\|gt.eq epsilon.alt)\|f_n - f\|\|g\|d x + integral_(\|f_n - f\|< epsilon.alt)\|f_n - f\|\|g\|d x\
   & lt.eq parallel f_n - f parallel_(L^2) integral_(\|f_n - f\|gt.eq epsilon.alt)\|g\|d x + epsilon.alt parallel g parallel_(L^2)$)

]
#statement("Exercise")[
Suppose $f\(z\)$ is a holomorphic function on the unit disk with $\|f\(z\)\|lt.eq 3$ for all $\|z\|< 1$, and $f\(1\/2\)= 2$. Show that $f\(z\)$ has no zeros in the disk $\|z\|< 1\/8$. (Hint: first show $f\(0\)eq.not 0$)

]
#proof(title: "Solution")[
Consider $phi.alt = frac(z - 1 / 2, 1 - 1 / 2 z)\,psi = frac(z - 2 / 3, 1 - 2 / 3 z)$, $g = psi compose f\/3 compose phi.alt^(- 1) in "Aut"\(bb(D)\)$,then $g\(0\)= 0$, by Schwartz lemma, $\|g\(z\)\|lt.eq\|z\|$, thus $g\(- 1\/2\)eq.not - 2\/3 arrow.l.r.double f\(0\)eq.not 0$, also it is easy to show that $phi.alt\({\|z\|< 1\/8 }\)subset.eq {\|z\|< 10\/17 }$, and $10\/17 < 2\,3$, $f$ has no zeros in the disk $\|z\|< 1\/8$

]
#statement("Exercise", title: [umd analysis qual 2021 January, problem 1])[
Does there exist a Lebesgue integrable function $f :\[0\,+ oo\)arrow.r bb(R)$ which is nowhere differentiable and such that the following function $F :\[0\,+ oo\)arrow.r bb(R)$ #display-equation($F\(x\)= integral_0^x f^2021 d m$) where $m$ represents Lebesgue measure on $\[0\,+ oo\)$, is well defined and infinitely differentiable? Provide an example or disprove its existence

]
#proof(title: "Solution")[
Suppose such an $f$ exists, then $f$ is smooth since $F$ is, assume $f$ is not constantly zero, then $f = root(2021, F)$ at where $f$ is nonzero which is smooth, giving a contradiction

]
#statement("Exercise", title: [umd analysis qual 2021 January, problem 2])[
Let $f\(z\)$ be an entire function on $bb(C)$, and suppose there is a constant $lambda > 0$ such that for all $z in bb(C)$, $\|Re f\(z\)\|gt.eq lambda\|Im f\(z\)\|$. Show that $f$ must be constant

]
#proof(title: "Solution")[
Pick $w_0 in.not {\|Re z\|gt.eq lambda\|Im z\|}$, say $i$, then $frac(1, f - w_0)$ is bounded. $\|f - i\|^2 gt.eq frac(lambda^2, 1 + lambda^2) > 0$

]
#statement("Exercise", title: [umd analysis qual 2021 January, problem 3])[
Let $0 < alpha < 1$. Verify the existence of a Lebesgue measurable set $A subset.eq\[0\,1\]$, with the property that for every open interval $\(a\,b\)subset\[0\,1\]$, we have $m\(A inter\(a\,b\)\)= alpha m\(\(a\,b\)\)$? Provide an example or disprove its existence

]
#proof(title: "Solution")[
Suppose such an $A$ exists, Let $G supset.eq A$ be a $G_delta$ set with $m\(G_delta\\A\)< delta$, since $G_delta = union.big I_i$ then we have #display-equation($m\(G_delta\)- delta & < m\(A\)= sum m\(A inter I_i\)= sum alpha m\(I_i\)= alpha m\(G_delta\)arrow.r.double m\(A\)lt.eq m\(G_delta\)< frac(delta, 1 - alpha)$) Since $delta$ can be arbitrarily small, so $m\(A\)= 0$, giving a contradiction

]
#statement("Exercise", title: [umd analysis qual 2021 January, problem 5])[
Let ${ f_n } subset.eq L_m^1\(bb(R)\)$. Assume that the sequence $f_n$ converges to $f$ in $L_m^1\(bb(R)\)$. Furthermore, assume that there exists $M > 0$ such that for all $n = 1\,dots.h.c$, we have $parallel f_n parallel_2 lt.eq M$. Verify whether or not $f_n$ converges to $f$ in $L_m^p\(bb(R)\)$ for $1 < p lt.eq 2$

]
#proof(title: "Solution")[
Since ${ f_n }$ converges to $f$ in $L^1\(bb(R)\)$, there is a subsequence $f_(n_k)$ such that $f_(n_k) arrow.r f$ almost everywhere, thus #display-equation($integral_(bb(R))\|f\|^2 d m = integral_(bb(R)) liminf_k\|f_(n_k)\|^2 d m lt.eq liminf_k integral_(bb(R))\|f_(n_k)\|^2 d m lt.eq M^2$) hence $f in L^2\(bb(R)\)$, by interpolation, $f\,f_n in L^p\(bb(R)\)$ for $1 lt.eq p lt.eq 2$, since $f_n$ is uniformally bounded, we may assume $f_(n_k)$ also weakly converges to $f$, then $f_n arrow.r f$ in $L^1\(bb(R)\)inter L^2\(bb(R)\)$, again by intepolation, $f_n arrow.r f$ in $L^p\(bb(R)\)$ for $1 lt.eq p lt.eq 2$

]
#statement("Exercise", title: [umd analysis qual 2021 January, problem 6])[
Define the following function of a complex variable $z$ #display-equation($F\(z\)= integral_0^oo frac(x^(z - 1), e^x - 1) d x$) Show that $F\(z\)$ is a holomorphic function for $Re z > 2$. Prove that $F\(z\)$ admits an analytic continuation as a meromorphic function for $Re z > 0$ whose only pole is simple at $z = 1$. Compute the residue of $F\(z\)$ at $z = 1$

]
#proof(title: "Solution")[
Consider $integral_epsilon.alt^N frac(x^(z - 1), e^x - 1) d x$, we know that $F\(z\)$ is holomorphic for $Re z > 2$. Define #display-equation($G\(z\)= integral_0^oo frac(x^(z - 1), e^x + 1) d x$) Then #display-equation($F\(z\)- G\(z\)= 1 / 2^(z - 1) F\(z\)arrow.r.double (1 - 1 / 2^(z - 1)) F\(z\)= G\(z\)$) Note that $G\(z\)$ is holomorphic on $Re z > 0$ and $G\(1\)= ln 2$. Note that #display-equation($2^(z - 1) = e^(\(z - 1\)ln 2) = 1 arrow.l.r.double z - 1 = frac(2 k pi, ln 2) i$) $\(1 - 2^(1 - z)\)'\(1 + 2 k pi i\/ln 2\)= - ln 2$, hence $"Res"\(F\,1\)= - 1$

]
#aside("Remark")[
Note that $F\(z\)= zeta\(z\)Gamma\(z\)$, $G\(z\)= eta\(z\)Gamma\(z\)$, here $eta\(z\)$ is Dirichlet's eta function

]
