#statement("Exercise")[
Let $S subset.eq M\(n\,bb(F)\)\,\(overline(bb(F)) = bb(F)\)$ be a set such that $\[X\,Y\]= 0\,forall X\,Y in S$, then elements in $S$ can be diagonalized simultaneously

]#label("Pairwise commuting matrices can be diagonalized simultaneously")
#proof(title: "Solution")[
Suppose $0 eq.not V_lambda$ is the $lambda$-eigenspace of $X in S$, then for any $Y in S$, $X Y v = Y X v = lambda Y v$ for $v in V_lambda$, thus $Y V_lambda subset.eq V_lambda$, thus $V_lambda$ is an invariant subspace for all $Y in frak(g)$, since $Y$ are all semisimple, by induction we can write $V$ as a direct sum of $V_lambda$'s, and they are all invariant under any $Y in frak(g)$, we only need to show that all elements of $frak(g)$ can be diagonalized simultaneously on each $V_lambda$, if $X\|_(V_lambda) = 1_(V_lambda)$, then we are done, otherwise we can decompose it into smaller eigenspaces

]
#statement("Exercise")[
Let $V$ be a finite dimensional $bb(F)$ vector space with $overline(bb(F)) = bb(F)$, and $frak(g) lt.eq frak(g l)\(V\)$ be a toral Lie algebra, then $frak(g)$ is abelian. Moreover, $X in frak(g)$ can be diagonalized simultaneously

]#label("Finite dimensional toral Lie algebra is abelian and its elements can be diagonalized simultaneously")
#proof(title: "Solution")[
Suppose $a d\(X\)\|_(frak(g)) eq.not 0$ for some $X in frak(g)$, since $overline(bb(F)) = bb(F)$, there exists $0 eq.not Y in frak(g)$ and $lambda eq.not 0$ such that $a d\(X\)\(Y\)= lambda Y$, by Proposition #ref(label("nilpotent/semisimple implies ad-nilpotent/ad-semisimple"), supplement: none), $a d\(Y\)$ is semisimple, suppose $lambda_j\,X_j$ are the eigenvalues and linearly independent eigenvectors, then we have $X = sum c_j X_j$ with $c_j eq.not 0$, and $0 = a d\(Y\)\(lambda Y\)= a d\(Y\)a d\(X\)\(Y\)= - a d\(Y\)^2\(X\)= - a d\(Y\)^2 (sum c_j X_j) = - sum c_j lambda_j^2 X_j$, thus $c_j lambda_j^2 = 0 arrow.r.double lambda_j = 0$, but $0 eq.not lambda Y = a d\(X\)\(Y\)= - a d\(Y\)\(X\)= - a d\(Y\)(sum c_j X_j) = - sum c_j lambda_j X_j = 0$ which is a contradiction \ Now that we know $\[frak(g)\,frak(g)\]= 0$, use Lemma #ref(label("Pairwise commuting matrices can be diagonalized simultaneously"), supplement: none), we know all elements of $frak(g)$ are diagonalizable simultaneously

]
#statement("Exercise")[
Lie group homomorphism has constant rank

]#label("Lie group homomorphism has constant rank")
#proof(title: "Solution")[
Let $phi.alt : G arrow.r G'$ be a Lie group homomorphism, for any $g in G$, it suffices to show $upright(r a n k)\(d phi.alt\)_g = upright(r a n k)\(d phi.alt\)_1$, since $phi.alt\(g h\)= phi.alt\(g\)phi.alt\(h\)$, thus $phi.alt compose L_g = L_(phi.alt\(g\)) compose phi.alt$, $\(d phi.alt\)_g\(d L_g\)_1 = d\(L_(phi.alt\(g\))\)_1\(d phi.alt\)_1$, and left multiplications are isomorphisms, we have $upright(r a n k)\(d phi.alt\)_g = upright(r a n k)\(d phi.alt\)_1$

]
#statement("Exercise")[
Let $G$ be a Lie group, $M\,N$ be smooth manifolds with a $G$ action, and $G$ acts transitively on $M$, for any equivariant map $f : M arrow.r N$, $f$ has constant rank

]
#proof(title: "Solution")[
For any $x in M$, denote $y = f\(x\)$, it suffices to show $upright(r a n k)\(d f\)_x = upright(r a n k)\(d f\)_(g x)$ since $G$ acts transitively on $M$, note that $f\(g x\)= g f\(x\)$, thus $f compose L_g = L_g compose f$, $\(d f\)_(g x)\(d L_g\)_x = d\(L_g\)_y\(d f\)_x$, and group actions are isomorphisms, we have $upright(r a n k)\(d f\)_x = upright(r a n k)\(d f\)_(g x)$

]
