#statement("Exercise")[
$G$ is a topological group, $A$ is called #strong[symmetric] if $A = A^(- 1)$

+ Topology of $G$ is translation invariant, $U$ is open $arrow.r.double$ $x U\,U x$ are open

+ $e in U$ is a neighborhood, then $e in V subset.eq U$ a symmetric neighborhood

+ $e in U$ is a neighborhood, then $e in V subset.eq V V subset.eq U$ with $V$ being a symmetric neighborhood

+ $H lt.eq G$ is a subgroup, then so is $macron(H)$

+ Open subgroups of $G$ are also closed(closed groups are not necessarily open, consider ${ e }$)

+ $K_1\,K_2 subset.eq G$ are compact sets, so is $K_1 K_2$

+ Suppose $G$ is a connected, $U$ is a neighborhood of $1$, then $G = chevron.l U chevron.r$

]#label("rudimentary facts about topological groups")
#proof(title: "Solution")[
+ Multiplication by $x$ is an isomorphism with $x^(- 1)$ being its inverse

+ Take $U inter U^(- 1)$

+ Since the multiplication $G times G arrow.r G$ is continuous, consider the preimage of $U$ which contains $V_1 times V_2$, take $V subset.eq V_1 inter V_2$ symmetric

+ If $x_alpha arrow.r x$, $y_beta arrow.r y$, then $x_alpha^(- 1) arrow.r x^(- 1)$, $x_alpha y_beta arrow.r x y$, since these maps are continuous. From this we know that $macron(H) = inter.big F$ wehre $F$ runs over all closed subgroup containing $H$

+ Suppose $H lt.eq G$ is open, then $H = G\\union.big_(x eq.not e) x H$ is closed, thus if $G$ is connected, then $H = G$

+ $K_1 K_2$ is the image of $K_1 times K_2$ under multiplication

+ By b, we there is a symmetric neighborhood $1 in V subset.eq U$, let $V_k$ be the subset of elements can be written in the product of no more the $k$ elements in $V$, then $V_1 = V$, $V_k = V_1 V_(k - 1)$ is open by induction, $chevron.l V chevron.r = union.big_(k = 1)^oo V_k$ is also open, by e, $G$ is generated by $V$ hence by $U$, and if $G$ is not connected, $G_0 = chevron.l V chevron.r$ is called the identity component of $G$

]
#statement("Exercise")[
$G$ is a topological group, if $G$ is $T_1$, then $G$ is Hausdorff, if $G$ is not $T_1$, then $H := overline({ e })$ is normal subgroup, $G\/H$ is a Hausdorff topological group

]
#proof(title: "Solution")[
If $G$ is $T_1$, according to Exercise #ref(label("rudimentary facts about topological groups"), supplement: none), for $x eq.not y$, $exists e in V V subset.eq U$ with $V$ a symmetric neighborhood of $e$ disjoint from $y^(- 1) x$, then $x V inter y V = nothing$, suppose $z = x v_1 = y v_2$, then $y^(- 1) x = v_2^(- 1) v_1 in V V$ thus reaches a contradiction

According to Proposiontion #ref(label("rudimentary facts about topological groups"), supplement: none), $H = inter.big H_i$, $H_i$ runs over closed subgroups of $G$, thus $H$ is the smallest closed subgroup, if $H$ is normal, otherwise $x H x^(- 1) inter H$ is a smaller closed subgroup for some $x$

In $G\/H$, identity is closed, by invariance of topology under translation, every point is closed, meaning $G\/H$ is $T_1$ thus Hausdorff

Checking $G\/H$ is still a topological group: $g in union.big_x x H$ open in $G$, then $g^(- 1) in (union.big_x x H)^(- 1) = union.big_x H^(- 1) x^(- 1) = union.big_x H x^(- 1) = union.big_x x^(- 1) H$

If $V times W arrow.r V W subset.eq union.big_x x H$, then $v w in union.big_x x H$, $forall v in V\,w in W$, then $forall h in H$, $v h w = v w w^(- 1) h w in union.big_x x H$, therefore, $V H times W H arrow.r V H W H subset.eq union.big_x x H$, notice that $V H$ is open as long as $V$ is open since $V H = union.big_(h in H) V h$

]
