+ Show that $B C_(A\,B) = C_(A\,B) B$ iff $A B = B A$

]
#proof(title: "Solution")[
+ $B = P D P^T$ for some orthogonal matrix $P$ and digonal matrix $D = "diag"\(d_1\,dots.h.c\,d_n\)$ with $d_i > 0$, then #ref(label("BC+CB=A"), supplement: none) becomes #display-equation($D P^T C P + P^T C P D = P^T A P$) Denote as #display-equation($D C' + C' D = A'$) Then we have #display-equation($\(d_i + d_j\)c'_(i j) = a'_(i j) arrow.r.double c'_(i j) = frac(a'_(i j), d_i + d_j)$) Therefore $C'$ is uniquely determined, so is $C = P C' P^T$

+ We only need to prove $D C' = C D'$ iff $A' D = D A'$. But $A' D = D A'$ is equivalent to $D^2 C' = C' D^2$. Therefore it suffices to prove $d_i c'_(i j) = d_j c'_(i j)$ iff $d_i^2 c'_(i j) = d_j^2 c'_(i j)$, i.e. $\(d_i - d_j\)c'_(i j) =\(d_i + d_j\)\(d_i - d_j\)c'_(i j)$ which is obviously true since $d_i + d_j > 0$

]
#statement("Exercise")[
Let $n$ be a positive integer, and all matrices are supposed to be over the real numbers

+ Let $A$ be a symmetric positive definite matrix of order $n$. Show that there exists a unique symmetric positive definite matrix $R$ such that $R^2 = A$. We denote such $R$ by $sqrt(A)$

+ Let $B$ be a nonsingular matrix of order $n$. Show that an orthogonal matrix $Q$ that maximizes $f\(Q\)= "tr"\(Q B\)$ satisfies #display-equation($Q = sqrt(B^T B)^(- 1) B^T = B^T sqrt(B B^T)^(- 1)$)

+ Let $G$ and $H$ be symmetric positive definite matrices of order $n$. Find a square matrix $L$ that minimizes #display-equation($g\(L\)= "tr" {\(I - L\)G\(I - L\)^T }$) subject to $L G L^T = H$

]
#proof(title: "Solution")[
+ $A = P D P^T$, here $P$ is an orthogonal matrix, $D = "diag"\(lambda_1\,dots.h.c\,lambda_n\)$, $lambda_i > 0$, take $R = P sqrt(D) P^T$ where $sqrt(D) = "diag"\(sqrt(lambda_1)\,dots.h.c\,sqrt(lambda_n)\)$, and the uniqueness of $R$ is obvious

+ Consider polar decomposition $B = sqrt(B B^T)\(sqrt(B B^T)^(- 1) B\)=\(B sqrt(B^T B)^(- 1)\)sqrt(B^T B)$, $sqrt(B B^T)\,sqrt(B^T B)$ are symmetric and positive definite, $sqrt(B B^T)^(- 1) B\,B sqrt(B^T B)^(- 1)$ are orthogonal, then $"tr"\(Q B\)= "tr"\(\(Q B sqrt(B^T B)^(- 1)\)sqrt(B^T B)\)= "tr"\(\(sqrt(B B^T)^(- 1) B Q\)sqrt(B B^T)\)$. Hence the problem can be rewritten as given a symmetric positive definite matrix $B$, the orthogonal maximizer of $f\(Q\)= "tr"\(Q B\)$ is $Q = I$, and by writing $B = P D P^T$, $"tr"\(Q B\)= "tr"\(P^T Q P D\)$, we may even assume $B = D$ is diagonal, but then $"tr"\(Q D\)= sum q_(k k) d_k$ which obtains maximum iff $q_(k k) = 1$ since $\|q_(k k)\|lt.eq 1$, this justifies our simplified version

+ $L G L^T = H$ can be rewritten as $Q^T Q = I$ with orthogonal matrix $Q = sqrt(H)^(- 1) L sqrt(G)$, note that #display-equation($"tr"\(\(I - L\)G\(I - L\)^T\)= "tr"\(G + H\)- "tr"\(L G\)- "tr"\(G L^T\)= "tr"\(G + H\)- 2 "tr"\(L G\)$) Hence we only need to maximize $"tr"\(L G\)= "tr"\(sqrt(H) Q sqrt(G)\)= "tr"\(Q sqrt(G) sqrt(H)\)$, by 2. we know #display-equation($Q = sqrt(H) sqrt(G) sqrt(sqrt(G) H sqrt(G))^(- 1) arrow.r.double L = H sqrt(G) sqrt(sqrt(G) H sqrt(G))^(- 1) sqrt(G)^(- 1)$)

]
#statement("Exercise")[
Consider the heat equation with Neumann's boundary condition: #display-equation($cases(u_t - Delta u = 0\,quad upright("in ") Omega times bb(R)^(+), frac(partial u, partial n) = 0\,quad upright("on ") Gamma times bb(R)^(+), u\(x\,0\)= v\(x\)\,quad upright("in ") Omega)$) #strong[\(a)] Show that $overline(u\(t\)) = overline(v)$ for $t gt.eq 0$, where $overline(v) = frac(1, \|Omega\|) integral_Omega v\(x\)d x$ denotes the average of $v$

#strong[\(b)] Show that $parallel u\(t\)- overline(v) parallel arrow.r 0$ as $t arrow.r oo$

]
#proof(title: "Solution")[
#strong[\(a)] By divergence theorem, we have #display-equation($0 = integral_Omega u_t - Delta u = integral_Omega u_t + nabla 1 dot.op nabla u - integral_(partial Omega) frac(partial u, partial n) = integral_Omega u_t = (integral_Omega u)_t$) Hence $integral_Omega u = integral_Omega v arrow.r.double overline(u) = overline(v)$

#strong[\(b)] By divergence theorem, we have #display-equation($0 = integral_Omega u u_t - u Delta u = 1 / 2 (integral_Omega u^2)_t + integral_Omega\|nabla u\|^2 arrow.r.double 1 / 2 (integral_Omega u^2)_t = - integral_Omega\|nabla u\|^2 lt.eq 0$) Hence $integral_Omega u^2 lt.eq integral_Omega v^2$. On the other hand, we have #display-equation($0 & = integral_Omega\(u_t - Delta u\)^2\
 & = integral_Omega u_t^2 - 2 u_t Delta u +\(Delta u\)^2\
 & = integral_Omega u_t^2 - 2 nabla u_t dot.op nabla u +\(Delta u\)^2\
 & = integral_Omega 2\(Delta u\)^2 + (integral_Omega \| nabla u \|^2)_t\
$) Which implies $integral_Omega\(Delta u\)^2 = - 1 / 2 (integral_Omega \| nabla u \|^2)_t$, thus #display-equation($(integral_Omega \| nabla u \|^2)^2 = (integral_Omega u Delta u)^2 lt.eq integral_Omega u^2 dot.op integral_Omega\(Delta u\)^2 lt.eq integral_Omega v^2 dot.op integral_Omega\(Delta u\)^2 = - 1 / 2 integral_Omega v^2 dot.op (integral_Omega \| nabla u \|^2)_t$) Denote $phi.alt := integral_Omega\|nabla u\|^2$ which is a function of $t$, $C := 1 / 2 integral_Omega v^2$, then the above equation becomes #display-equation($phi.alt^2 lt.eq - C phi.alt' arrow.r.double 0 gt.eq phi.alt^2 + C phi.alt' arrow.r.double 0 gt.eq 1 + C phi.alt' / phi.alt^2 = (t - C / phi.alt)'$) Which implies #display-equation($t - frac(C, phi.alt\(t\)) lt.eq - frac(C, phi.alt\(0\)) arrow.r.double frac(C, phi.alt\(t\)) gt.eq t + frac(C, phi.alt\(0\)) gt.eq t arrow.r.double phi.alt\(t\)lt.eq C / t$) Thus $phi.alt\(t\)arrow.r 0$ as $t arrow.r oo$

Now apply Poincaré's lemma, we get #display-equation($parallel u - overline(v) parallel_(L^2) = parallel u - overline(u) parallel_(L^2) lt.eq C parallel nabla u parallel_(L^2) arrow.r 0\,t arrow.r oo$)

]
#statement("Exercise")[
#display-equation($frac(d, d r) (1 / lr(|partial B \( x \, r \)|) integral_(partial B\(x\,r\)) u \( y \) d S) & = frac(d, d r) (frac(1, \|partial B\(0\,1\)\|) integral_(partial B\(0\,1\)) u \( x + r z \) d S)\
 & = frac(1, \|partial B\(0\,1\)\|) integral_(partial B\(0\,1\)) frac(d, d r) u\(x + r z\)d S\
 & = frac(1, \|partial B\(0\,1\)\|) integral_(partial B\(0\,1\)) z dot.op nabla u\(x + r z\)d S\
 & = frac(1, \|partial B\(x\,r\)\|) integral_(partial B\(x\,r\)) nu dot.op nabla u\(y\)d S\
 & = frac(1, \|partial B\(x\,r\)\|) integral_(B\(x\,r\)) Delta u\(y\)d y\
 & = r / n frac(1, \|B\(x\,r\)\|) integral_(B\(x\,r\)) Delta u\(y\)d y\
$) #display-equation($frac(d, d r) (integral_(B\(x\,r\)) u \( y \) d y) & = frac(d, d r) integral_0^r (integral_(partial B\(x\,s\)) u \( y \) d S) d s\
 & = integral_(partial B\(x\,r\)) u\(y\)d S$)

]
