#statement("Exercise")[
Consider the differential equation #numbered-equation($frac(partial, partial t) x\(n\,t\)= x\(n - 1\,t\)+ x\(n + 1\,t\)- 2 x\(n\,t\)$)#label("d/dt x(n,t)=x(n-1,t)+x(n+1,t)-2x(n,t)") for a function $x\(n\,t\)$ of an integer $n$ and real number $t gt.eq 0$. Assume that the function $x\(n\,t\)$ satisfies #numbered-equation($x\(n + N\,t\)= x\(n\,t\)$)#label("x(n+N,t)=x(n,t)") for any integer $n$, where $N$ is an integer larger than or equal to $3$. Furthermore, let $e\(m\,n\)= exp (i frac(2 pi m n, N))$ for integers $m$ and $n$

+ Let $f_m\(t\)$ be a function of a real number $t gt.eq 0$ for an integer $m$ with $f_m\(0\)= c_m$, where $c_m$ is a complex number. Assume that the function of the form $x\(n\,t\)= e\(m\,n\)f_m\(t\)$ satisfies differential equation #ref(label("d/dt x(n,t)=x(n-1,t)+x(n+1,t)-2x(n,t)"), supplement: none) and #ref(label("x(n+N,t)=x(n,t)"), supplement: none). Find $f_m\(t\)$

+ Let $\(g_0\,dots.h.c\,g_(N - 1)\)$ be an $N$-dimensional complex vector. Under the initial condition $x\(n\,0\)= g_n$, $n = 0\,dots.h.c\,N - 1$, find the solution of the differential equation #ref(label("d/dt x(n,t)=x(n-1,t)+x(n+1,t)-2x(n,t)"), supplement: none) with condition #ref(label("x(n+N,t)=x(n,t)"), supplement: none)

+ Find $lim_(t arrow.r oo) x\(n\,t\)$ for the solution $x\(n\,t\)$ found in $2 .$

]
#proof(title: "Solution")[
+ Note that $e\(m\,n + N\)= e\(m\,n\)$, hence #ref(label("x(n+N,t)=x(n,t)"), supplement: none) is justified. Plug $x\(n\,t\)= e\(m\,n\)f_m\(t\)$ in #ref(label("d/dt x(n,t)=x(n-1,t)+x(n+1,t)-2x(n,t)"), supplement: none), we have #display-equation($e^(i frac(2 pi m n, N)) f'_m\(t\)= (e^(i frac(2 pi m\(n - 1\), N)) + e^(i frac(2 pi m\(n + 1\), N)) - 2 e^(i frac(2 pi m n, N))) f_m\(t\)$) Which can be simplified as #display-equation($f'_m\(t\)= (e^(- i frac(2 pi m, N)) + e^(i frac(2 pi m, N)) - 2) f_m\(t\)= - 4 sin^2 (frac(pi m, N)) f_m\(t\)= K_m f_m\(t\)$) Solve this with initial condition $f_m\(0\)= c_m$ we get $f_m\(t\)= c_m e^(K_m t)$

+ Consider $x\(n\,t\)= sum_(m = 0)^(N - 1) e\(m\,n\)f_m\(t\)$, then $x\(n\,0\)= sum_(m = 0)^(N - 1) e\(m\,n\)f_m\(0\)= g_n$ which can be uniquely solved since $E = { e\(m\,n\)}_(0 lt.eq m\,n < N)$ is a Vandermonde matrix, suppose the solutions are $f_m\(0\)= c_m$, then use 1. to solve $f_m\(t\)$

+ Note that $K_m lt.eq 0$ for $0 lt.eq m < N$ and $K_m = 0$ iff $m = 0$. Let $omega = e^(frac(2 pi i, N))$, then $omega^N = 1$ and the determinant of #display-equation($mat(delim: "[", 1, 1, dots.h.c, 1; omega, omega^2, dots.h.c, omega^(N - 1); omega^2, omega^4, dots.h.c, omega^(2\(N - 1\)); dots.v, dots.v, dots.down, dots.v; omega^(N - 1), omega^(2\(N - 1\)), dots.h.c, omega^(\(N - 1\)^2))$) without the $j + 1$-th row would be #display-equation($ & omega dots.h.c hat(omega^j) dots.h.c omega^(N - 1) product_(0 lt.eq p < q lt.eq N - 1\
  p\,q eq.not j) (omega^q - omega^p)\
   & =\(- 1\)^j omega^(frac(N\(N - 1\), 2) - j) frac(product_(0 lt.eq p < q lt.eq N - 1) (omega^q - omega^p), product_(k eq.not j) (omega^k - omega^j))\
   & =\(- 1\)^j omega^(frac(N\(N - 1\), 2) - j) frac(det E, omega^(j\(N - 1\)) product_(k eq.not 0) (omega^k - 1))\
   & = frac(\(- 1\)^j omega^(frac(N\(N - 1\), 2)), N) det E$) thus by Cramer's rule we have #display-equation($lim_(t arrow.r oo) x\(n\,t\)= c_0 & = mat(delim: "|", g_0, 1, dots.h.c, 1; g_1, omega, dots.h.c, omega^(N - 1); dots.v, dots.v, dots.down, dots.v; g_(N - 1), omega^(N - 1), dots.h.c, omega^(\(N - 1\)^2)) / mat(delim: "|", 1, 1, dots.h.c, 1; 1, omega, dots.h.c, omega^(N - 1); dots.v, dots.v, dots.down, dots.v; 1, omega^(N - 1), dots.h.c, omega^(\(N - 1\)^2))\
   & = frac(sum_(j = 0)^(N - 1)\(- 1\)^j g_j frac(\(- 1\)^j omega^(frac(N\(N - 1\), 2)), N) det E, sum_(j = 0)^(N - 1)\(- 1\)^j frac(\(- 1\)^j omega^(frac(N\(N - 1\), 2)), N) det E)\
   & = frac(g_0 + dots.h.c + g_(N - 1), N)$)

]
